---
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find(
  (entry) => entry.slug === "ion-point-defence-2026-11-08",
);
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <section>
    <h2>01 / A small turret that turns to meet the threat</h2>
    <p>
      Ion Point Defence becomes the game’s default defence: the reference
      fitting every new ship starts with and every other defence is measured
      against. It sits in a weapon slot, turns towards the closest target and
      fires a light ion shot. The shot belongs to the Light Ion Cannon’s family,
      a thin blue instant ray, with half its reach and, as a starting
      assumption, half its damage. Like the cannon it fires automatically,
      without a button.
    </p>
    <p>
      The central tension is legibility against restraint. The player should see
      the turret working: its round base turning as one solid object that
      carries the barrel and housing with it, a moment of alignment, then the
      shot. Yet a baseline fitting is always present on a small hull, so it must
      not compete with flight or with the ship’s character. Whether a base of a
      few pixels still reads as rotating at flight scale, and what turning speed
      feels deliberate rather than snapping or sluggish, are open questions.
    </p>
    <p>
      This cycle will settle the turret’s form, how its rotation reads, its shot
      and its baseline behaviour: reach, firing rhythm, turning speed and which
      target counts as closest. The supplied concept with the round base is the
      likely form; the version without one is a comparison. Combat balance,
      energy cost and the future of the existing red laser turret remain
      adjacent questions.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/studies/ion-point-defence-2026-11-08/concept.png"
        alt="Supplied concept: the Light Ion Cannon for scale, a point-defence turret with a dark round base under its barrel and pale housing, and the same turret without the base"
      >
      <figcaption>
        Supplied concept. Left, the accepted Light Ion Cannon for comparison;
        centre, the turret on its round base; right, the turret without a base.
        No world scale has been selected.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>
  <section><h2>02 / To be developed after 01</h2></section>
</StudyLayout>

<style>
section > p {
  max-width: 72ch;
}
figure {
  margin: var(--spacing-lg) 0;
}
img {
  display: block;
  width: auto;
  max-width: 100%;
  height: 420px;
  object-fit: contain;
  background: var(--color-void);
}
figcaption {
  margin-top: var(--spacing-md);
  max-width: 72ch;
  color: var(--color-text-muted);
}
</style>
